// A callable read back out of a *mutable* tuple keeps its provable storage
// identity. This is a regression pin for `xxir_effect_check.inc.c`: the field
// receiver is the operand's value node, and `TUPLE_NEW` records the alias that
// reaches the tuple's contents node, so the origin walk no longer stops at the
// field read.
//
// The shape matters because class construction stages field initial values
// through mutable local slots and a tuple, so a callable field takes this path
// too — that cross-function half is still blocked, see
// `escaping_parameter_stored_into_field.xr`.
//
// Task340 §3.1 rule 1 lets an `escaping fn(...)` parameter outlive the call, and
// rule 3's prohibitions apply only to non-escaping values, so storing a callable
// in an aggregate is legal. `zero` below is a plain static function reference and
// carries no loans at all.
fn zero() -> i64 {
    return 0
}

fn main() -> i64 {
    var t = (zero, 1)
    const f = t.0
    return f()
}
